# Flow-manager browser-turn monitoring (binding)

This file is the canonical tracked manager-facing carrier for Browser-GPT
launch, observation, attribution, retry, publication, cleanup, diagnosis, and
handoff mechanics. The portable operator procedure is
[`docs/browser-gpt-turn-runbook.md`](../../docs/browser-gpt-turn-runbook.md).
Workflow and role rules remain in the canonical skills; CLI and result-schema
reference remains in [`scripts/chatgpt-browser-turn/README.md`](../../scripts/chatgpt-browser-turn/README.md).
Applicable long-running launcher internals remain in
[`docs/flow-manager-long-running-child-runbook.md`](../../docs/flow-manager-long-running-child-runbook.md).

## Launch and observation

For tracked create-issue and pack-review turns, use
`npm run chatgpt-browser-turn -- turn ...`. Applicable long-running manager
turns use `npm run flow-manager-browser-gpt-long-run -- ...` and its handoff
receipt. Before every tracked turn, the caller/orchestrator mints and retains one
non-empty `--invocation-id` and passes that exact value through the ordinary
transport or long-running adapter and every later harvest/finalization action
for the same attempt. Transport and adapter layers validate and forward this
identity; they must not mint, replace, or reinterpret it. Presence of
`--invocation-id` is common to ordinary and direct-publication-capable flows and
never selects direct mode by itself; direct mode requires its direct-only
arguments.

Before launch, use a trusted current checkout, the configured local headed
automation browser, and the applicable workflow/role. One invocation opens one
dedicated owned tab, sends one exact user payload, and observes that same tab.
Page/DOM completion is sufficient once the final assistant reply is stable and
no longer generating; PID, shell state, silence, log growth, service-terminal
witnesses, and heartbeats are not completion authority.
In a fresh worktree, copy `local.config.json` from the operator checkout into
the same worktree path, or set both required values before launch:
`export DISCUSS_WITH_GPT_PROJECT_URL="<PROJECT_URL>"` and
`export DISCUSS_WITH_GPT_CHROME_USER_DATA_DIR="<CHROME_USER_DATA_DIR>"`.
The `discuss-with-gpt: operator configuration missing` signature means the
mandatory configuration is not resolved; it can also occur with a copied but
incomplete config. Verify both `projectUrl` and `chromeUserDataDir`, or both
environment-variable equivalents.

The transport-owned `OPKTURNV1...` marker is inserted per payload. Exactly one
current user node with that marker proves ownership and page-path delivery;
zero or multiple matches fail closed. Product ids and prompt-text matching are
diagnostic only. Do not fabricate the marker in a prompt or use a second monitor.
The helper emits `turn-result/v1`; the long-running adapter additionally owns
the terminal envelope and handoff receipt. A direct event is invocation-local,
must be retained and reported, and must not gate siblings or the account.

## Stage and retry boundary

Stage cardinality and order belong to the canonical create-issue workflow and
are not redefined here. Relay or adjudication starts
only after every launched invocation is terminal, no retry runs or remains
eligible, and frozen revision checks match. Partial, blocked, and incident
attempts remain ordered evidence.

The fixed preparation topology is: T1 = one GPT lens; T2 = three concurrent
GPT architectural-review slots, then one GPT lens; T3 = three concurrent GPT
competitive slots only when the flow-manager/architect records genuine need,
then three concurrent GPT architectural-review slots, one Claude lens, and one
GPT lens. Concurrent browser starts are staggered by 10–15 seconds. T3 stages
run in that order, and a journaled competitive-stage skip is legal. These
counts replace older single-source or configurable-cardinality wording.

One paced retry under the same slot and `stageAttemptId` is legal only for a
proven pre-send quota/composer/fill failure with `send_count: 0`. It uses
`attemptOrdinal: 2` and `retryAttempt: true`, consumes the only retry, and may
settle blocked/exhausted. A possible or confirmed send, post-send failure,
ambiguous delivery, output conflict, missing terminal result, or
`send_count: 1` forbids resend. A zero-send result with unused eligibility
keeps the attempt unsettled until retry or explicit abandonment.

## Publication and tab lifecycle

Direct target-Issue publication and receipt-only manager output follow the
canonical create-issue workflow. Final output publication must succeed before
the exact retained invocation page may close. After send with no publication,
preserve the reachable page; release only this browser client. A lost page
receives no later close authority. Never close or commandeer a foreign,
sibling, or orphan tab by URL, target id, age, focus, metadata, or liveness.

For an eligible direct `turn-result/v1` carrying
`post_settlement_target.disposition: preserved_after_settlement`, the operator
**MUST** perform this sequence exactly once:

1. retain the complete direct final result;
2. preserve the byte-verified probe export result and harvested file;
3. verify `preserved_after_settlement` and that the helper made no close attempt;
4. prevent reuse, navigation, or send on the target during harvest and close;
5. invoke `npm run browser-gpt-post-settlement-close --` with the same configured profile/CDP namespace and those exact artifacts; and
6. record the structured `browser-gpt-post-settlement-close/v1` result before advancing.

The explicit command is the only later close authority. `closed` and
`already_absent` are terminal cleanup outcomes; launcher envelopes and every
ineligible, uncertain, or other non-`closed` path remain manual browser-UI
cleanup. They cannot authorize resend, close retry, target substitution,
compatibility inference, or harvest deletion.

## Observation loss and incident attribution

Missing `turn-result/v1`, `page_or_browser_lost_after_send`,
`fresh_conversation_landing_mismatch`, `owned_conversation_identity_mismatch`,
or an owned turn with no returned result is not proof of non-delivery.
Non-delivery is proven only by observing the conversation and finding no user
node containing the owned prompt. After post-send observation loss, re-resolve
the current target from the conversation URL, continue sanctioned observation,
and harvest the answer with the exact caller-owned `--invocation-id` for that
attempt. A saved target id may be stale and is not authoritative. Escalate only
after the probe shows no delivery or sanctioned observation is unavailable.
Every blocker names the exact probe and its observed result. Probe mechanics
remain owned by Issues #1272 and #1122.

A failure belongs to the current work only when it is reproducible and
causally linked to the current head/diff. Clean-base reproduction,
`stale_digest_pin`, and live-fleet `snapshot_changed` for `${OPK_BASE}`,
`${WAKE_STATE}`, or temporary class fences are not current-work evidence.
Rerun once after fleet quiescence; report a current-work blocker only if it
still reproduces and remains head/diff-causal. Do not rebind `TMPDIR` or
`OPK_BASE_DIR` after failure outside the sanctioned
`scripts/lib/vitest-live-store-harness.mjs` flow to make a guard pass without
frozen `OPK_VITEST_PRODUCTION_TMP` /
`OPK_VITEST_PRODUCTION_OPK_BASE` roots and an active production-store guard.
The existing `applyOpkVitestHarnessEnv` redirection is valid isolation.

## Legacy state and diagnostic probe

`status/list`, `clear`, capability/Gate-B, `possible_delivery`, profile walls,
stale recovery/orphan records, locks, queues, leases, and shell liveness are
not admission or completion authority. Unexpected events are appended
best-effort to the local recurrence journal and included in the current report;
do not use that journal to authorize work.

When direct diagnosis is genuinely needed, run the sanctioned one-shot page
probe with local variables such as `${CDP_ENDPOINT}` and an exact target
identity. `list`, `inspect`, `export`, and `liveness` are diagnostic-only and
always carry `workflow_authority: none`; they never publish a recovered turn or
progress a workflow. `harvest` is the sole action-producing probe
(`diagnostic_only: false`) and also carries `workflow_authority: none`; it may
recover/publish only the exact owned turn for the supplied invocation identity.
The explicit `inspect --url ... --open-if-missing true` acquisition path may
create one owned page, observe it until readiness, and close exactly that owned
page before returning. Do not replace these paths with raw CDP, Playwright,
salvage scripts, selectors, JavaScript, or a watch loop.

For contract-evidence authoring, `binding-id` uses
`producer:datum:expected`, for example
`orchestrator-pack:$.status:closed`. The producer must be listed in the closed
`scripts/contract-evidence-producer-registry.json`; repo-owned examples include
`orchestrator-pack` and `orchestrator-pack-scripts`. This is authoring
guidance, not validator documentation.

## Same-PR maintenance and handoff

Keep this carrier, the runbook, workflow skills, and implementation reference
synchronized in the same PR:

| Changed contract | Same-PR documentation obligation |
| --- | --- |
| launch, observation, attribution, retry, publication, cleanup, probe, handoff | update this carrier and audit the runbook |
| CLI option, result field, or component boundary | update the transport README; update carrier/runbook when operator behavior changes |
| role, direct publication, receipt, or invocation identity | update the owning skill and corresponding runbook template |
| local configuration or setup prerequisite | update the runbook and owning reference |
| link or Issue authority supersession | repair all in-scope pointers and current-authority wording |

Duplicate recoverable text is not a reason to reintroduce global recovery/clear
machinery. Source-preserving receipts and relay verification govern what may
progress.

## Sanctioned one-shot page probe

When direct page diagnosis or exact-owned-turn harvest is genuinely needed, use
only the canonical one-shot utility:

```bash
npm run browser-gpt-page-probe -- list --cdp http://127.0.0.1:9222
npm run browser-gpt-page-probe -- inspect --cdp http://127.0.0.1:9222 --target-id <exact-target-id>
npm run browser-gpt-page-probe -- inspect --cdp http://127.0.0.1:9222 --url <exact-conversation-url> --open-if-missing true
npm run browser-gpt-page-probe -- liveness --cdp http://127.0.0.1:9222
npm run browser-gpt-page-probe -- export --cdp http://127.0.0.1:9222 --target-id <exact-target-id> --role assistant --ordinal <n> --message-id <id> --representation innerText --expected-byte-length <bytes> --expected-sha256 <sha256> --output <new-file>
npm run browser-gpt-page-probe -- harvest --cdp http://127.0.0.1:9222 --profile <configured-profile> --invocation-id <caller-owned-invocation-id> --output <new-file>
```

The exact URL form remains existing-target-only unless `--open-if-missing true`
is explicitly supplied. Without acquisition it reuses exactly one normalized
match without creating or closing anything. With explicit acquisition it may
create one page, waits for exact normalized conversation identity and bounded
readable SPA state, then closes only the valid target identity returned by that
same invocation. A redirect or same-path cross-host result is `surface_unknown`
with `conversation_identity_mismatch`. Duplicate matches, malformed or timed-out
creation, cleanup failure, and every reused-page path fail closed without
guessed or delayed cleanup.

`liveness` is a single passive census with immediate `MAX_TARGETS` fan-out and
independent target deadlines. It reports responsive, unresponsive, or
unavailable rows and exact unresponsive IDs; it never creates, closes,
navigates, sends, reloads, or infers process death. Every operation runs once
and exits with no watch loop, daemon, retry, background owner, or retained
state.

The four diagnostic-only operations (`list`, `inspect`, `export`, `liveness`)
have no workflow authority:

- `generation_in_progress: false` is not completion evidence;
- node counts, hashes, witnesses, liveness rows, acquisition metadata, and an exported node file do not authorize publication, completion, retry, resend, cleanup, admission, recovery, review capture, or stage progression;
- use inspection output to select exactly one node representation for optional offline export; never request or construct a transcript dump;
- exported text may be sensitive. The caller supplies a new path in an existing directory and owns protection and cleanup of the resulting file.

`harvest` is different only in that it may recover and publish the exact owned
turn identified by the supplied invocation; its output remains
`workflow_authority: none` and therefore cannot itself advance a stage, grant a
resend, or authorize unrelated cleanup.

Do not replace this command with raw CDP WebSocket scripts, Playwright snippets,
`salvage-*.mjs`, caller-supplied selectors/JavaScript, a watch loop, or a second
supported probe.

## Forbidden duplicate monitoring and authority

- No independent 5–10 or 10–15 minute watchdog layered over the helper.
- No periodic browser-tab sweeper that closes tabs it did not open.
- No reporting "still running" from shell/PID/log state alone.
- No probe-driven takeover, helper bypass, resend, or workflow progression.
- No flow-manager consolidation or content judgment over sibling reviewer output.

The one-shot page probe diagnoses an already-open page, may explicitly acquire
one page under the zero-match ownership contract, or may harvest only the exact
owned turn for a caller-supplied invocation. It is not a fallback state machine
or browser-turn owner.

## Session handoff

Never end a flow-manager turn with only “background job running”. Report the
Issue and revision, the caller-owned invocation id, the owned chat URL when
known, the helper result or latest direct observation, the
`reviewEpisodeId`/`stageAttemptId`/slot when applicable, any direct incident
surfaced by the helper, and the next legal flow action.